Two selectors wxWidgets sends that were not here, and each one killed the application.

AN UNRECOGNIZED SELECTOR IS NOT A DEGRADED ANSWER IN A wxWidgets APPLICATION. It raises, wx catches
it with catch(...), cannot name it, and prints Unhandled unknown exception; terminating the
application. There is no ObjC Terminating app line and the process exits 0, so from outside it looks
like the user quit.

Money Manager EX did exactly that when its Currency Manager panel was built, reproducibly:

  cider: UNRECOGNIZED -[NSLayoutManager setTypesetterBehavior:]
  cider: UNRECOGNIZED -[wxDVCNSTableColumn setHidden:]
  Unhandled unknown exception; terminating the application.

NSLayoutManager setTypesetterBehavior: and typesetterBehavior. The behaviour lives on the typesetter,
which already had both accessors and the enum; what was missing is the convenience macOS puts on the
layout manager in front of it, so these forward. DECLARED IN NSTypesetter.h, not NSLayoutManager.h,
because NSTypesetterBehavior is declared in the former and the former is what imports the latter.
Declared the other way round the compiler answers expected a type, which it did.

NSTableColumn setHidden: and isHidden. A hidden column is still in the table, it is only not drawn,
so this is a flag rather than a removal, and the setter tiles the table when the flag changes.
wxWidgets subclasses NSTableColumn as wxDVCNSTableColumn and sends it.

MEASURED, and only what was measured: the terminating exception is GONE, 1 to 0, and both
UNRECOGNIZED lines are gone, 2 to 0, on the same scenario.

WHAT IS NOT FIXED, stated plainly: the application still exits on that click. It now does so
SILENTLY, with no exception and no unrecognized selector, and the Currency Manager window is not
created at all where before it was created twice. That is a different and quieter cause on the same
path, and task #243 carries it.

Gates: run-dts-batch, buck-bundled-patch-record-check, roster-sweep and roster-input.

